WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.990
Yes, you can look through a
dictionary, read through a dictionary.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:07.470
It's kind of fun, but my
experience is we don't retain much.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.240
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here
today to talk about dictionaries

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.110
and language learning.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.790
If you enjoy my videos, please subscribe,
click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:23.430
Uh, if you follow me on a podcast
service, um, which is a good thing

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.070
to do if you're learning English,
because you can then study the lessons

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.870
on LingQ, but do leave a comment.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:28.740
I do appreciate it.

00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:34.199
So dictionaries, I am a sucker for
buying books and buying books on

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:39.060
things that are of interest to me,
such as history, but such as language.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.550
So I have bought a lot of dictionaries.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.129
Way back when I was at school or when I
was learning French or learning Chinese,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.830
we needed dictionaries, we needed...

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.540
and I got Chinese dictionaries.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:55.680
This isn't, I got many, many more I just
don't know where they are right now.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.980
So starting with Chinese, lots
of dictionaries, and that's kind

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.180
of the only choice we had then.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.140
There were, there were no online
texts with online dictionaries.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.360
The dictionary, the Chinese dictionary had
the big disadvantage and to some extent

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.260
it shares it with other dictionaries
that it's difficult to look words up,

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:18.150
it's time consuming, uh, even a, sort
of a, a, a dictionary that's in the

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.700
Latin alphabet or in an alphabet where,
you know, the order of the letters.

00:01:22.245 --> 00:01:26.205
Uh, and therefore you can look
things up based on that alphabet.

00:01:26.205 --> 00:01:27.195
That's easier.

00:01:27.555 --> 00:01:30.825
When I first started learning
Chinese, we either had to know the

00:01:30.825 --> 00:01:35.745
stroke, the number of strokes in the
character, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13.

00:01:35.865 --> 00:01:39.975
And then go look for that character, you
know, in that section of the dictionary.

00:01:40.035 --> 00:01:44.175
And if we counted the strokes wrong,
which as a beginner you're likely

00:01:44.175 --> 00:01:48.555
to do, then you never found it or it
took forever, or they wanted you to

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.445
figure out the radical, the component.

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:55.560
And then characters would be organized in
the dictionary based on these components.

00:01:56.399 --> 00:02:03.509
Uh, that again could be, uh, a little
difficult, but what is common to whether

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:08.370
you're looking up Chinese characters in
a dictionary or looking up words based

00:02:08.375 --> 00:02:12.959
on the alphabet uh, the problem is that
very soon after you close the dictionary,

00:02:12.959 --> 00:02:14.630
you have forgotten the meaning.

00:02:15.109 --> 00:02:20.525
Now I should point out that Chinese
dictionaries now tend to rely on pinyin.

00:02:20.625 --> 00:02:27.825
In other words, the phonetic script, the
Latin alphabet-based phonetic script, uh,

00:02:27.855 --> 00:02:31.845
so that you can more easily look things
up, but still the problem remains that

00:02:31.845 --> 00:02:35.295
once you close the dictionary very quickly
you're gonna forget what you looked up.

00:02:35.745 --> 00:02:40.065
Therefore, a few things become
important when it comes to dictionaries.

00:02:40.425 --> 00:02:44.235
Number one, you have to have a
purpose for looking a word up.

00:02:44.715 --> 00:02:49.860
So typically I look a word up now
if I'm reading or list reading a, a

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.540
particular, you know, piece of content,
and I want to know what that word means.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:55.680
So I look it up.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:57.060
I get it.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.570
Hopefully I haven't forgotten it by the
time I get back to the text that I'm

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:04.680
reading, that's where online dictionaries
are so good because you get the meaning

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.630
right away before you forget the meaning.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:10.049
Um,  so a purpose.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:14.730
And I, I remember as a, as a child
playing Scrabble with my, with my father

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:18.870
or with my brother or my mother, uh, we
would look words up in a dictionary to

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.299
see if that word was legal in Scrabble.

00:03:22.395 --> 00:03:23.835
so there's a specific purpose.

00:03:23.895 --> 00:03:25.275
Didn't even bother with the meaning.

00:03:25.275 --> 00:03:29.535
I might have glanced at the
meaning, but I wanted to confirm

00:03:29.655 --> 00:03:31.435
that that was a legal word.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:32.475
So there's a purpose.

00:03:32.865 --> 00:03:34.905
So you need to have a purpose
for looking things up.

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:38.505
I'll get into later this idea
of kind of viewing words in a

00:03:38.505 --> 00:03:41.235
dictionary as a means of learning
words, which I'm not a big fan of.

00:03:41.925 --> 00:03:43.035
Uh, so you have a purpose.

00:03:43.185 --> 00:03:47.565
The second thing is it should be
as easy and effective as possible.

00:03:48.674 --> 00:03:52.005
When I use a dictionary online,
or even before that, you know,

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:57.105
Traditional dictionary um, I
want it to come to my language.

00:03:57.135 --> 00:03:58.875
I don't use monolingual.

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:02.745
In other words, target language to
target language dictionaries, unless I'm

00:04:02.745 --> 00:04:07.215
very advanced in the language because
I don't wanna find a meaning for that

00:04:07.215 --> 00:04:11.774
word or a definition, which contains
a lot more words that I don't know.

00:04:11.894 --> 00:04:12.285
Okay.

00:04:12.375 --> 00:04:14.954
And I have bought a lot
of dictionaries over time.

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:15.814
I should show you these.

00:04:15.814 --> 00:04:18.644
So I've shown you a couple
of Chinese dictionaries here.

00:04:19.034 --> 00:04:25.365
Uh, Korean learners, Korean English,
Handbook of Korean Vocabulary.

00:04:25.544 --> 00:04:28.844
Again, dictionary vocabulary,
it's all part of the same thing.

00:04:29.325 --> 00:04:33.255
Uh, what did I, and for every
language that I study, I get these

00:04:33.255 --> 00:04:34.724
dictionaries and I never look at them.

00:04:35.325 --> 00:04:38.565
Frequency dictionary, I thought, wow,
if I can learn the most, you know,

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:40.425
frequent words, that's gonna help me.

00:04:40.425 --> 00:04:45.044
In fact, it doesn't because the most
frequent words show up a lot anyway, you

00:04:45.044 --> 00:04:49.695
don't need to find them in a dictionary
ordered, you know, by order frequency.

00:04:50.145 --> 00:04:56.685
So money spent to satisfy an itch,
but didn't really amount to very much.

00:04:57.045 --> 00:05:00.045
Uh, I have Farsi phrase book,
which I don't look at very much.

00:05:00.555 --> 00:05:07.425
Um, you know, I bought an Arabic-English
bilingual dictionary, full of pictures.

00:05:07.455 --> 00:05:08.115
Okay.

00:05:08.115 --> 00:05:09.735
Haven't really used that very much.

00:05:10.325 --> 00:05:14.115
Uh there's this 15 minute
Arabic, learn in just 12 weeks.

00:05:14.115 --> 00:05:16.425
So 12 weeks, you don't
know much I can assure.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:20.640
And it's more or less the same
pictures and words in Arabic.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.750
Uh, yeah, whatever Spanish English for...

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.960
in fact, the beginning of LingQ was
when I was reading books in Spanish

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:31.410
and there were so many words I
didn't know, on every page 10 or so.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.630
And so I was thumbing
through the dictionary.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.810
And then in the end, I figured that was
not very efficient, beacause I would

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:41.610
forget them or I would list them all
to look up later on and forget them.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:43.890
So that was my Spanish English dictionary.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.420
I've even bought a number of dictionaries
where I go from one language that I'm

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:53.610
learning into another language, say
Czech to, to Russian to kind of see

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.110
how, where they're similar or different.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:06:02.190
As you thumb through the,
this ... there are some aha moments,

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:04.200
but mostly it's not that effective.

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:07.200
I don't retain much and I
don't do that much anymore.

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:11.760
So getting back then to this idea of the
dictionary and how to make it useful.

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:16.920
Yes you can look through a dictionary,
read through a dictionary is kind of

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:22.380
fun, but my experience is we don't
retain much or put it this way and I

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:25.480
should add actually a dictionary is just
a collection of words with meaninig.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:30.570
I prefer bilingual, so from the
target language to a language that

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:35.550
I know very, very well, uh, but it
can be a dictionary of medical terms.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.730
It can be a dictionary of,
uh, agricultural terms.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:42.900
It can be simply a vocabulary list
at the end of a chapter in a book.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:43.980
It's the same idea.

00:06:43.985 --> 00:06:46.080
It's a list of words with meaning.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:51.030
Uh, and so it's part of this
whole language learning process.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.320
So I think we should kind of separate...

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:59.610
and now of course, dictionaries have
become online and there are sort of,

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:03.750
uh, flashcard systems and Anki, and
there's a whole variety of things

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:08.430
that have been built around this idea
of acquiring vocabulary or helping

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:12.450
us acquire vocabulary from our basic
reading and listening activities.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:18.179
But the basic way we learn vocabulary is
through those listening and reading, uh,

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:26.310
activities so that the dictionary or other
vocabulary learning assists are simply our

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:34.349
minor um, systems or resources that help
us learn the vocabulary, which we learn

00:07:34.349 --> 00:07:37.080
primarily from our listening and reading.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:44.219
So I do use these, uh, resources I use
online dictionaries, obviously, uh, at

00:07:44.219 --> 00:07:48.419
LingQ for every language there's, uh, an
option of three or four or five online

00:07:48.419 --> 00:07:50.349
dictionaries that we can use with LingQ.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:56.820
Some of them for certain languages offer
you conjugating uh, conjugation tables

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:58.200
for the word that you're looking up.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:04.080
Uh, some of them offer, you know, help
you with, uh, writing Chinese characters.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:07.140
Uh, so there's any number
of other resources that are

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:09.659
bunched with these dictionaries.

00:08:10.109 --> 00:08:14.039
And so that makes them even
more useful as resources, uh,

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:15.870
in terms of review activities.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:19.380
As I've said before, I
prefer these matching pairs.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:23.940
I've said that any kind of review
activity should deal with a small

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:30.270
number of words or phrases, you should
review them soon after seeing them in an

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:36.090
actual context and the activity itself
should be as easy as possible to do so.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:40.980
That's why typically I will go through a
text in Persian, one sentence at a time

00:08:40.985 --> 00:08:46.824
review, six or seven terms using matching
pairs, which is the easiest thing to do.

00:08:47.275 --> 00:08:50.814
Uh, and I do them right
away, easy and not too many.

00:08:51.204 --> 00:08:56.935
And so that is kind of my approach
to using these vocabulary lists as

00:08:56.935 --> 00:08:59.395
a way to acquire more vocabulary.

00:09:00.025 --> 00:09:04.625
I really don't spend a lot of time reading
the dictionary, however it's possible

00:09:04.625 --> 00:09:06.780
to simulate reading the dictionary.

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:10.650
The advantage of reading a dictionary,
if it's organized alphabetically, is

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:14.850
that you can see words that have the
same prefix and that can sometimes

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.829
help you connect one word to another.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:18.760
And we can do that at LingQ.

00:09:18.785 --> 00:09:22.980
I can simply, uh, organize
my, a vocabulary list.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.640
Say the overall vocabulary list
or vocabulary list are words that

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.615
I'm close to knowing, which is my
status three, or from a particular

00:09:30.615 --> 00:09:34.574
lesson and I can organize them
by, uh, uh, in alphabetical order.

00:09:34.665 --> 00:09:36.765
So I can see related words.

00:09:37.185 --> 00:09:42.974
Uh, I think everyone has to sort of design
their own approach to using dictionaries

00:09:42.979 --> 00:09:48.995
and vocabulary review activities, to help
them you know, to help accelerate the

00:09:48.995 --> 00:09:51.604
process of acquiring words and phrases.

00:09:51.604 --> 00:09:53.824
I should add again, at
LingQ, you can save phrases.

00:09:53.824 --> 00:09:54.935
That's also very important.

00:09:55.474 --> 00:10:00.125
Uh, but by and large, if you review
these, like I mentioned that I had a

00:10:00.395 --> 00:10:04.625
somewhere here at Persian phrase book,
when you review phrases or words, it

00:10:04.625 --> 00:10:08.584
helps if they are already familiar
to you so that the, the first time

00:10:08.584 --> 00:10:13.230
you encounter a word in a dictionary,
it's to deal with a specific need.

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.240
What does it mean in this context?

00:10:15.569 --> 00:10:19.980
But thereafter when you are reviewing
them, I find that the more familiar you

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:24.510
are with these starting to get a handle
of these, of the meaning of these words,

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:28.290
the more likely you're gonna benefit
from the vocabulary review activities,

00:10:28.295 --> 00:10:30.000
including reading a dictionary.

00:10:30.385 --> 00:10:34.974
If you know many of the words in this
specific dictionary, chances are reviewing

00:10:34.974 --> 00:10:41.425
it is gonna help, you know, really, uh,
confirm your understanding of these words.

00:10:41.905 --> 00:10:45.454
Uh, but uh, if you encounter a word for
the first time in a dictionary, you're,

00:10:45.474 --> 00:10:48.175
you're not very likely to remember it.

00:10:48.175 --> 00:10:50.365
And you're certainly not
gonna have a good sense of the

00:10:50.365 --> 00:10:51.834
scope of meaning of that word.

00:10:52.015 --> 00:10:54.805
So just a bit of a ramble
on the role of dictionaries.

00:10:54.805 --> 00:10:58.950
I'd be interested in hearing your views
on the subject and I'll leave you uh,

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:02.850
videos that I have done on the subject
of vocabulary and dictionary in the past.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:04.020
Thank you for listening.

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:04.530
Bye for now.

